</table>

<h2>The exclusions that quietly extend the window</h2>
<p>Section 12(2) of the Limitation Act excludes the day on which judgment was pronounced and the time requisite for obtaining a certified copy of the judgment or order appealed from. In practice, this means the clock largely pauses between the date the copy application is filed and the date the copy is ready. Two habits protect the exclusion: apply for the certified copy immediately — ideally the same day — and preserve the copy application with its endorsement of filing and readiness dates, because the appellate registry computes &ldquo;time requisite&rdquo; from precisely those endorsements. Delay in applying for the copy is not excluded; only the time the copying department itself takes.</p>

<h2>Condonation: indulgence with discipline</h2>
<p>Section 5 of the Limitation Act applies to criminal appeals, and the test — sufficient cause — is applied with a justice-oriented tilt. But the jurisprudence has hardened in recent years, and three working rules emerge from how courts actually decide these applications.</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Explain the whole period.</strong> An affidavit that accounts for the first month of delay but goes silent on the next three invites rejection. The chronology should run date-by-date from the judgment to the filing.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Custody is context, not a formality.</strong> For incarcerated appellants, courts accept the real-world difficulties of instructing counsel and arranging papers — but the application should say what was actually done and when, rather than reciting incarceration as a mantra.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The State gets no special latitude.</strong> Government appeals routed through layers of approval are a recurring source of long delays, and courts have grown visibly less tolerant of file-movement explanations unaccompanied by any sense of urgency.</div>
</div>

<h2>A computing checklist before filing</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Fix the forum first — Sessions Court or High Court — because the forum fixes the period (30 or 60 days).</p>
  <p>Diarise the outer date from the day of pronouncement, then adjust for the certified-copy exclusion once the copy is ready.</p>
  <p>For acquittal matters, identify who is appealing: the State&rsquo;s 90-day appeal, and the complainant&rsquo;s special-leave application with its own 60-day or six-month window, run on different tracks.</p>
  <p>If the period has lapsed, prepare the condonation application with the same care as the appeal itself — supporting documents, medical records, copy-application endorsements — and file both together.</p>
  <p>For a convict in custody, remember the jail-appeal route under Section 424 BNSS as a safeguard against time running out while private arrangements are made.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">Limitation in criminal appeals is forum-specific and fact-specific: the periods above are the general scheme, but the correct computation in a given case depends on the precise order under challenge, the court it comes from and the dates on the certified-copy record. That computation should always be verified against the record before the last date is assumed.</div>
